# Ownership of the merge gate's own configuration, and of every other path
# the Gate file approval workflow treats as a gate file.
#
# Everything under .github/ is covered: the review workflow, the release
# workflow, the pinned review action reference, and this file itself. A change
# to any of them needs an approving review from an account the automation does
# not hold. Automation can author changes here; it cannot approve them.
#
# Why .github/: the review workflow runs from the pull request head on
# same-repo branches, so without this rule a pull request that weakens the
# reviewing logic is judged by the weakened logic it just introduced.
#
# The path after .github/ is the rest of the gate pattern in
# .github/workflows/gate-file-approval.yml: the script pr-review.yml runs to
# decide what the reviewer is handed. A code-owner review is an approval no
# workflow run can produce, so it carries the same rule as .github/. Keep
# this list equal to that pattern.
#
# These are the ONLY paths in the repository that require a human approval.
# Ordinary pull requests are gated by checks alone, and that is deliberate: a
# reviewer who has to approve everything approves nothing.

# Changes under these paths are authored by the repository's automation
# identity, never by the code owner: an author cannot approve their own pull
# request, so a change authored by the sole code owner would have no legal
# approver.

/.github/ @thebenignhacker
/scripts/pr-review-generated-files.mjs @thebenignhacker
